# Mobile — a list row pays every cost N times

A row component is not one component. With the render window in
[`listVirtualization.ts`](/apps/mobile/src/components/primitives/listVirtualization.ts) there are
roughly twenty of it mounted, and the whole set is torn down and rebuilt every time a media-type
chip, a tab, or a section selector swaps the list. React Native runs all of that on the one thread
that also handles the next tap, so per-row cost shows up as a stall between the tap and the next
paint — not as a slow spinner.

Before adding anything to a row, or to a component a row renders, ask what it costs times the
window, and again on every switch.

## Narrow the subscription before reaching for `React.memo`

`React.memo` cannot stop a re-render caused by `useContext`. A row that reads a broad context
re-renders on every unrelated change to it no matter how stable its props are, so the context split
comes first and the memo second.

- Rows read **`usePlaybackRow()`** (targets, enclosure params, `pause`, `playClipById`) or
  **`usePlaybackIsPlaying()`**. Not `usePlayback()` / `usePlaybackSession()` — that value is
  memoized over ~35 dependencies including `transportState`, so it changes on every play, pause, and
  rate change.
- The playhead (`usePlaybackProgressRatio` and friends) belongs to now-playing chrome only. A row
  never subscribes to something that ticks.
- Per-tick data subscriptions are opt-in and default off (`includeProgress = false`). See
  **mobile-progress-ux-and-notification-channels**.
- OS/environment state a row reads is one process-wide store behind `useSyncExternalStore`
  (`useReduceMotion`), not a listener per instance.

## Don't mount what the user has not asked for

- An overflow menu, sheet, or viewer mounts on **first open** and stays mounted after
  (`shouldMountRowMoreMenu`). A closed `AppOverlay` per row is real tree, real layout, real cost.
- A control that needs a record mounts only when the record exists (`resolveHomeFeedRowDownload`),
  rather than rendering an inert instance for every row.

## Keep props stable across the row boundary

Rows are wrapped in `React.memo`, which only pays off if the parent hands back the same references:

- Hoist styles, `renderItem`, `keyExtractor`, and option arrays to module scope or memoize them.
- Prefer one stable handler that receives the row over a closure minted per row; when a per-row
  closure is unavoidable, key it by id inside the row.
- Row data is fresh immutable literals per load and is never mutated in place, which is what makes
  memo comparison safe. Keep it that way.
- A new object or array literal in JSX props defeats memo for all N rows at once.

## One `StyleSheet` per theme, not per instance

Read styles through **`useThemedStyles`** ([`src/theme/useThemedStyles.ts`](/apps/mobile/src/theme/useThemedStyles.ts))
with a **module-scope** factory. `useMemo(() => StyleSheet.create(…))` caches per instance, so it
builds an identical stylesheet for every row; this caches on factory identity and theme object, so
the first row pays and the rest read. An inline arrow factory is a new cache entry every render —
never pass one.

Styles that depend on props take one of two shapes:

- **A factory per variant**, when the variants are a small fixed set (`Badge` tone,
  `MediaRowActions` appearance). Pick the factory before calling the hook.
- **Split static from dynamic**, when they are not (`Button`'s 6 variants × 4 sizes, safe-area
  insets, `isLast`, caller padding). Cache the theme-only chrome in the factory and compose the
  dynamic part at the call site in a style array.

## Bound text work to what is displayed

A feed description runs to kilobytes of markup and a row shows two lines of it. Use
**`htmlToPlainTextPreview`** from `@podverse/helpers` on any list or preview path; keep
`htmlToPlainText` for surfaces where the whole string can be read. Do not persist a second
pre-rendered column to avoid the work — that is a device data migration
(**dto-changes-are-device-data-migrations**) for a win the slice already gets.

## Switching lists is one interruptible load

A chip, tab, or section switch is the moment all of the above is paid, so the switch itself must not
add to it:

- Load **once** per switch. Wait for persisted filter/sort preferences to resolve before the first
  read instead of loading with defaults and again with the real prefs.
- Guard every `setState` after an await with a request-id ref so a superseded load cannot paint over
  the current one, and so the user can switch again mid-flight.
- Clear the list in the **same urgent commit** as the selector state. Holding the clear in a
  transition leaves the previous selection's rows mounted under the new chip, which reads as the
  wrong list loading. The clear is cheap; the stall is uncancelled load work, so cancel that.
- Show the list's loading state while the read is in flight (`VerticalCenter` + `ListLoading` in
  `ListEmptyComponent`). An empty list with no indicator reads as a broken screen.
- Keep loader identity out of the effect deps that trigger it, or a refresh re-triggers the initial
  load.

## What not to trade away

These are mechanical changes to how work is scheduled, not license to change what the user sees.
Style values stay byte-identical, `testID`s and `accessibilityLabel` / `accessibilityState` /
`accessibilityRole` stay exactly as they were, and affordances keep appearing where they did — a
lazily mounted menu still opens on the first press. Pick the verification tier with
**mobile-test-economics**, and when a claim is about speed, get the number from the perf harness
(`scripts/mobile/perf-report.mjs`, see **mobile-perf-measured-claims**) rather than asserting it.

Pay these costs down before proposing a structural list rewrite (FlashList, a new row architecture).
The cheap fixes are what make the structural question measurable.

